  evidence, not clean-path baselines.
- OBSTACLE 2 (measurement finding, red-team grade): on-path DPI selectively kills TLS ClientHellos to :8443
  by TLS stack fingerprint, in bursts:
    * Go crypto/tls: 75/75 fresh handshakes OK over 150s (hsmap probe 10:41:33-10:44:04, tls1.3, ~35ms)
    * OpenSSL s_client: 8/8 OK (incl 3 full HTTPS GETs)
    * Windows schannel (curl.exe/.NET/PS): 10/10 RST mid-handshake ("Recv failure: Connection was reset")
    * Android conscrypt/BoringSSL (OkHttp in app): intermittent — whole scenarios pass then fail in waves
  server journal shows "TLS handshake error ... connection reset by peer" FROM client IP 210.21.11.228
  => bidirectional RST injection by middlebox, not server. Same behavior via Clash egress and direct.
  Working hypothesis: CH-size/fingerprint-based (BoringSSL/schannel large padded+GREASE/PQ hellos killed;
  small Go/OpenSSL hellos pass). THIS IS EXACTLY THE INTERFERENCE CLASS ANEB EXISTS TO MEASURE.
